WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.035 --> 00:00:03.460
OK so, at the end of this last section,
I canceled our party,

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:05.960
and said that we have this algorithm
that

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.270
works really well in practice on
theoretical spectra.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:12.391
But the issue is going to be that when
we move to experimental spectra,

00:00:12.391 --> 00:00:13.274
we're going to have issues

00:00:13.274 --> 00:00:17.539
because these experimental spectra often
produce errors.

00:00:17.539 --> 00:00:20.374
Here are the theoretical

00:00:20.374 --> 00:00:24.570
and an experimental spectrum for the
peptide NQEL.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.430
The theoretical spectrum is on the top,
and then

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:30.670
we're giving you a hypothetical experimental
spectrum for the same peptide.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.500
This experimental spectrum highlights a
couple of different ways

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.050
that the experimental spectrum can be
flawed.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:40.700
It can detect “false” masses.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:44.190
These are masses that are present in the
experimental spectrum

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:48.230
but that don't correspond to real masses
in the theoretical spectrum.

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:50.989
Examples are 99 and 299 here.

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:52.780
We also have “missing” masses.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.550
They're masses that are in the
theoretical spectrum of the

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:58.220
correct peptide but that don't actually

00:00:58.220 --> 00:00:59.960
get produced in the experimental
spectrum,

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.458
so they're “missing”.

00:01:02.458 --> 00:01:06.250
This is a problem, because our current
implementation,

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:08.100
in order for it to consider a peptide

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.820
correct, the peptide's theoretical
spectrum has to exactly match

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:15.910
the experimental spectrum that we have,
and that's clearly not the case here.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:18.370
The question then is what do we do.

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:24.300
What we're going to do is, instead of
having to match the spectrum exactly,

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.920
we're going to instead “score” a peptide
based off

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:31.990
of how similar it is to the experimental
spectrum.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:35.040
For example, here,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.020
the theoretical spectrum of NQEL is still
pretty

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:42.390
similar to the experimental spectrum that
we have.

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.650
Notice that it shares 11 masses.

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:47.170
So, we're going to say that the score
then,

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:50.285
of NQEL, with this experimental spectrum,
is equal to 11.

00:01:50.285 --> 00:01:52.265
We need to use this scoring function

00:01:52.265 --> 00:01:54.795
to say certain peptides are better than
others

00:01:54.795 --> 00:01:58.558
and to use this to modify the branch and bound
algorithm

00:01:58.558 --> 00:02:01.804
that we have in order to make it a little
bit better.

00:02:01.804 --> 00:02:04.484
The idea that I want to use here is

00:02:04.484 --> 00:02:07.820
similar to something called a “cut” in a
golf tournament.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:12.030
Now I'm a huge golf fan, so maybe
I'm just partial to this idea,

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.080
but I thought it fit it pretty well.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.896
What the cut does, is it reduces the
field to

00:02:16.896 --> 00:02:20.900
only the players that are deemed to be in
contention for the tournament.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:23.240
So, if there are players, that are not
doing too

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:25.690
well after a couple of rounds, then, they
say,

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:27.810
OK, well, we're just going to let them go
home.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.700
And, we want to have the tournament only
for the

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.690
players who have a chance of actually
winning the tournament.

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:35.610
Here's a hypothetical leaderboard,

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.200
and we may say, in this hypothetical
small tournament that

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.020
we want to keep the top three players on
the leaderboard.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:47.100
Keeping in mind that low golf
scores are good, we put the cut line here.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:47.114
 

00:02:47.114 --> 00:02:49.692
But this would be unfair to this golfer,
right, because

00:02:49.692 --> 00:02:52.772
they're tied with the third place golfer,
and so we

00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:55.701
need to say, well we keep the top three
players…

00:02:55.701 --> 00:02:57.570
…as well as ties.

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:59.970
So, keep the top three players “with ties”.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:04.370
This is going to motivate the bound
step that we do.

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:07.909
So, after this cut,
we have four players remaining.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.467
The new idea is, we're going to call it
LeaderboardCyclopeptideSequencing.

00:03:14.467 --> 00:03:17.430
How it works is that we're going
to start off with a zero peptide.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.890
So, this is an empty string, essentially.

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:23.010
And then we're going to extend

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:24.610
each peptide on the leaderboard

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:28.350
in each of 18 possible different
directions, so we

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:31.580
add each of the 18 different amino acid
masses

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:34.980
to the peptides that we have, currently,
in the leaderboard, which

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:39.520
when we begin is empty, or it has just the
zero peptide.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:42.250
Then we're going to have this
“cut” step, which is the

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:46.670
new “bound step”, and it's going to cut the
low scoring peptide, so it says:

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:48.670
Assign everything a score,

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:51.798
see what the score of every peptide is,
and then cut the low scoring ones.

00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:53.850
Get them out of there so that we can

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:57.149
prevent the number of solutions
that we consider from growing,

00:03:57.149 --> 00:03:59.885
and so that we can keep only the high
scoring peptides.

00:03:59.885 --> 00:04:03.922
So, we're going to keep the top N
peptides “with ties”.

00:04:03.922 --> 00:04:08.240
Then, the next step is going to say OK,
we'll update the “leader peptide”.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.314
We're going to keep track of

00:04:10.314 --> 00:04:12.901
what the leader peptide should be,

00:04:12.901 --> 00:04:13.906
and, if there's

00:04:13.906 --> 00:04:17.471
a higher scoring peptide that we can find
on the leaderboard,

00:04:17.471 --> 00:04:20.271
so if something moves up and becomes a
higher scoring peptide,

00:04:20.271 --> 00:04:23.268
and its mass is equal to that parent
mass, that we can detect.

00:04:23.268 --> 00:04:24.721
By the “parent mass” I mean

00:04:24.721 --> 00:04:27.895
We said that we're always going to be able
to

00:04:27.895 --> 00:04:32.208
know in advance what the correct mass of
the entire peptide should be.

00:04:32.208 --> 00:04:35.888
So, we always want to check if these
candidate solutions

00:04:35.888 --> 00:04:38.928
that we have have a mass that's equal to
that

00:04:38.928 --> 00:04:39.837
total mass.

00:04:39.837 --> 00:04:41.741
And then, we're going to eliminate any

00:04:41.741 --> 00:04:43.914
peptides whose mass is going to grow too
big.

00:04:43.914 --> 00:04:46.202
So, if their mass exceeds the
parent mass,

00:04:46.202 --> 00:04:49.250
then, we're not going to want to expand
them any more, because

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:52.320
their mass is too big, and we want
to get rid of them, too.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:53.884
So, once we do these steps, we're just

00:04:53.884 --> 00:04:55.825
going to iterate these steps over and over
again.

00:04:55.825 --> 00:04:58.003
So, we have a branch step, and then, a
bound step.

00:04:58.003 --> 00:05:02.480
And then, we have an “update step”, where we
say, is there a leader?

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:03.960
And can we get rid of

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:06.200
anything whose mass has gotten too big?

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:07.910
At the end, after we go through this

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:11.290
procedure, we're going to branch and bound
and branch and bound,

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:16.790
it's actually going to guarantee to always
have an empty leaderboard at the end.

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:19.160
So, at step 6, after we go through

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:21.650
steps 2 through 5, we're going to have an
empty leaderboard.

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:25.290
And then, at that time we return, what is
our leader?

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:27.360
What's the best peptide that we've
encountered so far?

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:29.010
So, we have

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:30.250
this updated method.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:35.040
But, I do want to give you the warning,
that it's what's called a “heuristic”.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:39.950
Because it's dealing with high scoring
peptides, there's a chance that

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:44.230
there is an initially low-scoring peptide
that winds up eventually being

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:47.450
the highest scoring peptide, so that
eventually would be our leader,

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:50.110
but because we cut it in an early stage,
because, we said it's

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:54.200
not in the top however many N peptides,
then we throw it out.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:57.150
And so there is a possibility that
we throw out

00:05:57.150 --> 00:06:00.450
the correct peptide, or what will
eventually become the correct peptide,

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:02.050
at the expense of having a quick method.

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:05.700
And so for this reason it's a heuristic.

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:09.790
Let's test it though, because remember,
we're considering

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:12.890
how well these algorithms work in practice.

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:18.050
So, here is a hypothetical spectrum.
It has 10% false and missing masses.

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:19.360
I'll highlight where

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:20.930
the false and missing masses are.

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:23.750
So, here are the false masses, and here
are the missing masses.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.222
Now, we're not going to know which of

00:06:25.222 --> 00:06:27.013
these are false and missing to begin
with.

00:06:27.013 --> 00:06:29.639
In particular, we're not going to have the
missing masses present.

00:06:29.639 --> 00:06:30.308
That's the whole point.

00:06:30.308 --> 00:06:32.800
They're not present in the experimental
spectrum.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:34.720
So, we'll have this picture, but we won't

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:36.425
know which of the masses should be
green.

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:40.510
And then, we say, let's run our
algorithm, let's run

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:44.570
our leaderboard algorithm, on this
hypothetical data set, and see what it

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:45.520
churns out.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:49.440
And so it returns the, the correct
Tyrocidine B1

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:52.290
peptide, which is where this came from, so
we're happy.

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:56.340
Then we say, well, maybe 10% false and

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:59.360
missing masses, maybe that was a little
bit optimistic.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:02.640
So, let's introduce a slightly noisier
Tyrocidine B1

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:05.780
spectrum that has 25% false and missing
masses.

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:07.520
And again, I'll show you (even though we
won't know this)

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:09.620
I'll show you where the false and
missing masses are,

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:10.920
so you can believe me.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.220
And, we can say, we won't know which
masses are missing.

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:18.600
Now, when we run that same algorithm,
on this noisier spectrum.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:20.150
We're going to see that

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:23.271
the peptide that it produces

00:07:23.271 --> 00:07:28.730
is actually not Tyrocidine B1.
It's close, but instead of the

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:34.330
correct amino acid here, It's been
replaced with an AD, so this is bad.

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:38.230
In this case, we wouldn't be able to
reconstruct the correct peptide.

